sitive that business ultimately grew  to 1,100 people and was publicly traded  for eight years so it didn't hinder our  growth at all so my advice to this would  be restaurant tier on the reservation  was to go down to the bureau of indian  affairs building in town there were  there a bunch of people in the office  and it's a funny thing every one of them  gets hungry every day at lunch time I  encouraged him to go there on a Tuesday  and take orders for lunch on Thursday  and get deposits well  he used those deposits to buy groceries  and then made the lunches in his own  kitchen and delivered him on Thursday he  made three hundred dollars at first day  and that weekend he got a catering job  so again he used the deposit to buy the  groceries he needed he was cash flow  positive the first week not bad he hopes  to ultimately build his business up and  grow by a lunch truck so he can service  the various communities around his  reservation now the MBA would probably  still be writing the business plan  bootstrapping is how most startups are  formed less than 1% of business startups  in the u.s. raise professional money and  yet that's the prevailing wisdom amongst  business to school graduates all will  say you write a business plan and then  you go raise money and yet what they  don't say is that once you get the money  you start a bonfire you pitch the money  on the bonfire and you hope there's a  company there before you run out of  money it's a very high-risk way to go i  lecture a lot on entrepreneurship and  bootstrapping and I've written a book  called bootstrapping your business I've  mentored hundreds of Montana  entrepreneurs and the benefits of  bootstrapping are really tremendous  number one you end up building your  business on a legitimate real-world  value proposition from day one number  two you initiate the critical sales  learning process sooner because you  start with sales ultimately market sales  is just market research where you ask  for the or