sively for work in  government and so just to play the  reciprocal two out of 23 programs  produce people for the private sector  without entrepreneurs we would not have  any businesses without businesses we  would not have a private sector and  without the private sector or productive  industries as adam smith term them we  would not have economic surplus to pay  for government education the arts or  health care all that add quality to our  life our free society needs  entrepreneurs I'm a lifetime  entrepreneur i started in junior high  mowing lawns mostly because i wanted to  buy a dirt bike and i use the surplus  then to also purchase a Radio Shack  computer back in the 1970s and with that  trs-80 in high school I started my first  software company a post college as was  mentioned as Jack mentioned that kind  introduction I've started and grown five  technology businesses our latest  software firm rightnow technologies my  wife and I started in our home we  started it with  than five thousand dollars in startup  capital and we doubled revenue and the  number of employees every 90 days for  years all while being cash flow positive  in 2004 that business went public and we  are traded on Wall Street and ultimately  grew to 1,100 employees and was  ultimately purchased by Oracle we  bootstrapped that business using  customers money to grow it and we did  that with all of our other for  businesses as well and i'll talk a  little more about that later but the  important point here is it if we want a  vibrant private sector we need more  young people and people of all ages  aspiring to start businesses I've become  convinced that we over complicate  business startups not that starting  businesses is easy but it's really not  that complicated I recently got  acquainted with a program in Austin  Texas called the Acton Academy  children's business fair and what's  really curious about this program is  they start with six year olds and I  think the parents in the audience will  recognize the fact that that the beauty  of a six-year-old is that they don't  know anything's impossible we seem to  learn that later in life this children's  business fair actually has kids aged 6  to 14 starting businesses and there's  only three rules the first rule is you  must make something the second rule is  you must sell it to actual customers and  the third rule is you must charge more  than it costs at the end of the day  that's the basis of business oh but  there is a fourth rule no adult  supervision and these kids do it on  their own uncontrolled uncontrolled or  unconstrained by adult supervision you  see we really do over complicate  business they've been running this fair  for almost a decade and they have a  hundred student teams that participate  each year and some of these young kids  make over ten thousand dollars in a  weekend we need experiential  entrepreneurship programs like this in  all of our schools to plant the seed of  entrepreneurship in young minds I'm  convinced that entrepreneurship really  cannot be taught in a classroom  entrepreneurship is not a spectator  sport entrepreneurship can only be  learned experientially that's why  programs like the children's business  fair are so important I encourage those  of you who can to start one in your  communities details on this program are  up on the web you can just google  children's business fair now I really  want to drive home this idea that  entrepreneurship is can only be learned  experientially I had a young man come to  work for us when we were about 10  employees and he stayed with us until we  were about 800 employees so he got to  see the whole thing from a front row  seat he left and started his own  technology company and it grew to dozens  of employees so he enjoyed some success  he had experienced our business  firsthand and yet when he came back to  visit a few years later he told me that  he thought he knew what a start-up was  about but until he was actually in the  saddle making decisions day in and day  out he had no idea I remember what we  started our first business I felt like I  was doubling my knowledge about every  two weeks and that went on for years  until you're in the saddle doing it  yourself you really don't understand  what's involved you see ideas are easy  execution is really hard and you don't  really learn about it so you're doing it  again entrepreneurship cannot be taught  in a classroom it must be experienced so  in addition to giving our kids  entrepreneurial dreams and experiences  what else do we knew need to do to  preserve our free market society well we  need to look at higher education as well  I've  I've had the great privilege to lecture  at many top business schools here in the  US and in England and the students  always seem to like my talk but I  actually never been invited back and  it'll be interesting to see how today's  talk goes but I think I'm on higher  ground here at Hillsdale so this should  be okay I believe business schools face  to problems as they try to produce  entrepreneurs the first is they have  become very vocational in there near  exclusive focus on the mechanics of  business courses focus on market  research competitive strategy and  finance but little focus is placed on  the philosophy of business and how  wealth is actually created in our  society essentially what we're missing  is traditional economics can we bring  back Adam Smith as a result many  students have come to believe that  for-profit corporations are somehow evil  or at least less good now don't get me  wrong there are certainly some bad  actors in the private sector and in  corporate America too we could probably  hold a whole other lecture on crony  capitalism and and but that's not our  focus for today I was I was an  entrepreneur in residence last year at a  at a college and I gave a public  entrepreneurial talk in September and  then followed it up with a shark tank  the next night where the students were  able to pitch their ideas and we  crowdsource the best ideas and then I  went back on campus once a month to  coach these student teams through the  startup process and what really shocked  me was that these students really  believed that not-for-profit  organizations were somehow morally  superior to a for-profit enterprise and  this seems to be an increasingly  prevalent view on college campuses the  prevailing wisdom is that somehow  nonprofits are better than for-profit  enterprise and only when I led the stew  through a set of Socratic questions did  they realize that without for-profit  firms they'd have nothing to eat or no  cars to drive no homes to live in and  maybe most importantly no cell phones to  text on my final lecture of the year on  that campus there was on the virtue of  the private sector and the virtue of the  private sector was honestly a new  concept for many of the students that  actually some of the faculty as well and  with these views on college campuses as  today is it any big surprise that so  many young people are embracing Bernie  Sanders I'm encouraged that there's been  growing activity to create centers on  college campuses that promote positive  impact the positive impact of  free-market principles and that we have  places like hillsdale where free market  principles are still taught shouldn't  the virtue of the free market system  though be promoted to all our children  in all of our schools we must regain the  moral high ground in economics or we  will lose our economy we must promote  the virtue of free enterprise as the  only path to prosperity and individual  freedom so the second issue that I found  with business schools is they better  prepare students for work in large  corporations than they do to launch  startups you see if you're or can get a  large corporation you've been tasked  with launching a new division there's  going to be millions of dollars at risk  so by necessity most of the planning  work focuses on risk mitigation and  downside protection cash forecasts  competitive analysis contingency  planning all these sorts of things in  stark contrast when you're doing a  start-up in your garage you generally  have very little at risk so the best  thing to do is try stuff experiment  throw things against the wall and see if  it sticks nothing ventured nothing  gained this try and see startup mode is  exact opposite of what's taught in most  business schools and they're preparing  physically they're preparing kids for  the work in the corporate world this big  company think encourages entrepreneurs  to believe that the first thing they  must do is write a lengthy business plan  and they go raise money I recently had a  man come to me from one of our Indian  reservations in Montana because he  wanted to start a restaurant in his  community and Montana proudly boasts  seven indian reservations all across the  state I've had a chance to visit them  all and mentor entrepreneurs on many of  them and we greatly need more Economic  Opportunity in these communities so  anyway this gentleman comes up to me and  shares his story you want to start a  restaurant there were no other  businesses where he lived but he had  responded to my entrepreneurship ideas  in a seminar he had attended so he did  what most people do he asked me for  money to start his restaurant he wanted  to buy kitchen equipment and he won to  rent a building and so he'd start up his  restaurant well being a dyed-in-the-wool  both bootstrapper I told him there might  be a better way to start his restaurant  so I encouraged him to bootstrap now  bootstrapping is simply a concept of  using customers money to start a  business and as was mentioned I've  started five businesses this way with no  outside capital and lest you think  bootstrapping might slow growth in our  most recent business you know as I  mentioned we doubled revenue and the  number of employees every 90 days and  the whole time we were cash flow  positive that business ultimately grew  to 1,100 people and was publicly traded  for eight years so it didn't hinder our  growth at all so my advice to this would  be restaurant tier on the reservation  was to go down to the bureau of indian  affairs building in town there were  there a bunch of people in the office  and it's a funny thing every one of them  gets hungry every day at lunch time I  encouraged him to go there on a Tuesday  and take orders for lunch on Thursday  and get deposits well  he used those deposits to buy groceries  and then made the lunches in his own  kitchen and delivered him on Thursday he  made three hundred dollars at first day  and that weekend he got a catering job  so again he used the deposit to buy the  groceries he needed he was cash flow  positive the first week not bad he hopes  to ultimately build his business up and  grow by a lunch truck so he can service  the various communities around his  reservation now the MBA would probably  still be writing the business plan  bootstrapping is how most startups are  formed less than 1% of business startups  in the u.s. raise professional money and  yet that's the prevailing wisdom amongst  business to school graduates all will  say you write a business plan and then  you go raise money and yet what they  don't say is that once you get the money  you start a bonfire you pitch the money  on the bonfire and you hope there's a  company there before you run out of  money it's a very high-risk way to go i  lecture a lot on entrepreneurship and  bootstrapping and I've written a book  called bootstrapping your business I've  mentored hundreds of Montana  entrepreneurs and the benefits of  bootstrapping are really tremendous  number one you end up building your  business on a legitimate real-world  value proposition from day one number  two you initiate the critical sales  learning process sooner because you  start with sales ultimately market sales  is just market research where you ask  for the order and you can do that before  you have a product or service three  bootstrapping accelerates time to market  and time to profitability for you'll be  less likely to make big fatal mistakes  because you can't spend money you don't  have five you force yourself into  unconventional thinking because  necessity really is the mother of  invention six you have more freedom and  flexibility particularly related to exit  strategies from businesses and seven  you own much if not all of the business  you create in our last business even  after we went public the employees still  own seventy percent of the business this  created a lot of prosperity a lot of  great outcomes for a lot of people when  i was starting our last firm the first  thing i did was make four hundred phone  calls and back in the 90s when i was  getting started I had a suspicion that  the internet was disrupting how  companies deliver customer service so I  called managers of customer service and  ask them how their business was being  impacted by the internet and they mostly  said the same thing it's these emails  they said this marketing department put  up this website and they've been sending  all the customer service emails to me  and then they lower their voice and say  I'm not answering them because I don't  have any budget to do that well after a  few calls i formulated a proposition and  made more phone calls refining the  proposition as i talked to more people  after four hundred phone calls i had  zeroed in on the essence of a value  proposition that i knew people would pay  for at that point i actually started  writing the software I didn't start  programming until after I knew exactly  what the market wanted and six weeks  later I introduced the first product  customer service emails went down by  fifty to seventy percent for the  corporations that we served and I had 40  paying customers before I hired the  first employee besides Souza to myself  as I mentioned before we doubled revenue  and 